{"name":"Freefall","hex":"#565266","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"freefall","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of motion affected by no acceleration or force other than that of gravity.","A state of motion but allowing for the presence of incidental air resistance not caused intentionally by devices such parachutes or wings."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To drop in a state of freefall.","To decline or worsen rapidly and uncontrollably."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/freefall"}],"entries":[{"title":"Freefall (Nothing More song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Freefall\" is a song by American rock band Nothing More featuring Chris Daughtry of his eponymous band. It is the fourth single and bonus track from Nothing More's digital deluxe version of their seventh album, Carnal. It reached No. 1 on the Billboard Mainstream Rock Airplay chart in August 2025."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freefall_(Nothing_More_song)"},{"title":"Freefall (ride)","blocks":[{"text":"The Freefall is an amusement ride developed by Giovanola and marketed throughout the world by Swiss company Intamin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Freefall_at_SFOG.jpg/330px-Freefall_at_SFOG.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freefall_(ride)"},{"title":"Free fall","blocks":[{"text":"In classical mechanics, free fall is any motion of a body where gravity is the only force acting upon it.\nA freely falling object may not necessarily be falling down in the vertical direction. If the common definition of the word \"fall\" is used, an object moving upwards is not considered to be falling, but using scientific definitions, if it is subject to only the force of gravity, it is said to be in free fall. The Moon is thus in free fall around the Earth, though its orbital speed keeps it in very far orbit from the Earth's surface."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_fall"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Drop_time.jpg/500px-Drop_time.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Drop_time.jpg/500px-Drop_time.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Measured fall time of a small steel sphere falling from various heights. The data is in good agreement with the predicted fall time of \n  \n    \n      \n        \n          \n            2\n            h\n            \n              /\n            \n            g\n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\textstyle {\\sqrt {2h/g}}}\n  \n, where h is the height and g is the free-fall acceleration due to gravity.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrop_time.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Free-fall.gif/330px-Free-fall.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Free-fall.gif/330px-Free-fall.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Free-fall","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFree-fall.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/MeteorAccGraph.jpg/500px-MeteorAccGraph.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/MeteorAccGraph.jpg/500px-MeteorAccGraph.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Acceleration of a small meteoroid when entering the Earth's atmosphere 80 km high (above which the Kennelly–Heaviside layer) at different initial velocities of 35, 25 and 15 km/s. Air pressure and air density are height-dependent.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeteorAccGraph.jpg"}],"sounds":{"freefall":[{"ty